    "full": "5] ---\n5\nˆZa ˆZb ˆXa ˆXb |ψ ⟩\n+1 +1 (|gg⟩ + |ee⟩)/\n√\n2\n+1 −1 (|gg⟩ − |ee⟩)/\n√\n2\n−1 +1 (|ge⟩ + |eg⟩)/\n√\n2\n−1 −1 (|ge⟩ − |eg⟩)/\n√\n2\nTABLE II. Eigenstates of the two-qubit operators ˆZa ˆZb and\nˆXa ˆXb. The four eigenstates are the Bell states for this system.\nˆXb error on the second qubit will transform this state to\n(|ge⟩ + |eg⟩)/\n√\n2, which is also an eigenstate but with\neigenvalues ( −1, +1); the change in the ﬁrst eigenvalue\nwill signal this error. Note however that this error cannot be distinguished from an ˆXa error, which yields the\nsame ﬁnal state and ﬁnal eigenvalues. By contrast, a ˆZa\nerror on the ﬁrst qubit will transform ( |gg⟩+ |ee⟩)/\n√\n2 to\n(|gg⟩ − |ee⟩)/\n√\n2, which is an eigenstate with eigenvalues\n(+1, −1), changing the second eigenvalue (a ˆZb error will\ngive the same outcome as ˆZa). Hence, while errors can\nalways be detected, they cannot be uniquely identiﬁed:\nA more complex system is needed to achieve that, the\nsurface code being one example.\nThe operator products ˆXa ˆXb and ˆZa ˆZb are called stabilizers. Stabilizers are very important in preserving\nquantum states: By repeatedly measuring a quantum\nsystem using a complete set of commuting stabilizers, the\nsystem is forced into a simultaneous and unique eigenstate of all the stabilizers."
