    "full": "−⟩\n|egeg⟩ | − + − +⟩\n|eeee⟩ | − − − −⟩\n−1 |ggge ⟩ | + + + −⟩\n|ggeg ⟩ | + + − +⟩\n|gegg ⟩ | + − + +⟩\n|eggg ⟩ | − + + +⟩\n|geee⟩ | + − − −⟩\n|egee⟩ | − + − −⟩\n|eege⟩ | − − + −⟩\n|eeeg⟩ | − − − +⟩\nTABLE III. Eigenstates for the four-qubit stabilizers\nˆZa ˆZb ˆZc ˆZd and ˆXa ˆXb ˆXc ˆXd.\nple with all data and measurement qubits in their ground\nstates |g⟩.\nOnce selected, in the absence of errors, the same state\n|ψ⟩ will be maintained by each subsequent cycle of the\nsequence, with each measure qubit yielding a measurement outcome Xabcd or Zabcd equal to that of the previous cycle. This occurs because all ˆX and ˆZ stabilizers\ncommute with one another. This is trivial for stabilizers\nthat do not have any qubits in common, as ˆX and ˆZ operators on diﬀerent qubits always commute. Stabilizers\nthat have qubits in common will always share two such\nqubits, so every ˆX stabilizer shares two data qubits with\neach neighboring ˆZ stabilizer and vice versa. 9 Hence we\nhave, for an ˆX and ˆZ stabilizer that measure data qubits\na and b in common,\n[ ˆXa ˆXb ˆXc ˆXd , ˆZa ˆZb ˆZe ˆZf ]\n= ( ˆXa ˆZa)( ˆXb ˆZb) ˆXc ˆXd ˆZe ˆZf\n− ( ˆZa ˆXa)( ˆZb ˆXb) ˆXc ˆXd ˆZe ˆZf\n= 0 ,\n(3)\nas we get a minus sign from commuting ˆXa through ˆZa\nas well as one from commuting ˆXb through ˆZb. Note the\nsimilarity of this four-qubit stabilizer commutator with\nthe two-qubit stabilizer example in Eq."
