    "full": "e will see in a\nmoment. In the ﬁrst case the measurement outcomes are\nthe same as before the error, while in the second case the\nsigns of the two measure-X qubits adjacent to data qubit\na will change. This is apparent from the following:\nˆXa ˆXb ˆXc ˆXd\n(\nˆZa|ψ⟩\n)\n= − ˆZa\n(\nˆXa ˆXb ˆXc ˆXd|ψ⟩\n)\n= −Xabcd\n(\nˆZa|ψ⟩\n) (4)\n(recall that Xabcd is the measurement outcome of the\nmeasure-X qubit). This shows that ˆZa|ψ⟩ is an eigenstate\nof this ˆX stabilizer, but with the opposite sign from |ψ⟩.\nThe same result applies to the other measure-X qubit\nadjacent to a: ˆZa|ψ⟩ is an eigenstate of that stabilizer,\nwith opposite sign compared to |ψ⟩. The data qubit error will not change the outcomes of the two neighboring measure-Z qubits, as the ˆZ operators all commute:\n[ ˆZa ˆZb ˆZc ˆZd, ˆZa] = 0. In terms of the quiescent state,\nˆZa ˆZb ˆZc ˆZd\n(\nˆZa|ψ⟩\n)\n= ˆZa\n(\nˆZa ˆZb ˆZc ˆZd|ψ⟩\n)\n= Zabcd\n(\nˆZa|ψ⟩\n)\n,\n(5)\nclearly again an eigenstate but with unchanged sign.\nHence, in this second case, the state |ψ′⟩ = ( ˆIa +ǫ ˆZa)|ψ⟩\nis projected to ˆZa|ψ⟩, which is a diﬀerent eigenstate of\nall the stabilizers in the array.\nThe erroneous phase-ﬂip ˆZa causes sign changes in the\nmeasurement outcomes of the two measure-X qubits adjacent to qubit a, allowing us to detect and localize the\nphase-ﬂip error."
