    "full": "his operation is detected by the\nmeasure-Z qubit just to the right of the ﬂipped data\nqubit, but we can at the same time perform an ˆX2\noperation on the data qubit on the other side of that\nmeasure-Z. The ﬁrst ˆZ stabilizer now commutes with\nthe product of these two operations, but the measureZ qubit to the right of ˆX2 still reports a change; hence\nwe add a third operation ˆX3 to the set, and so on, until we have created a product of concurrent ˆX operations ˆXL = ˆX1 ˆX2 ˆX3 ˆX4 ˆX5, which connects the two X\nboundaries on the left and right sides of the array. Every\nmeasure-Z qubit now interacts with pairs of data qubits\nappearing in the ˆXL operator, so by construction the\nproduct chain ˆXL commutes with all the ˆZ stabilizers\nin the array (note ˆXL trivially commutes with the ˆX\nstabilizers). Hence, if the ˆXL operator is applied to a\nquiescent state |ψ⟩, generating the state |ψX ⟩ = ˆXL|ψ⟩,\nthe new state |ψX ⟩ will be a quiescent state with identical measurement outcomes to |ψ⟩. Note that |ψX ⟩ is\nnot equal to |ψ⟩, as we have bit-ﬂipped ﬁve data qubits\nin going from |ψ⟩ to |ψX ⟩, and as ˆXL cannot be written\nas a product of stabilizers, |ψX ⟩ is not trivially related\nto |ψ⟩ (unless |ψ⟩ is an eigenstate of XL, namely |+L⟩ or\n|−L⟩). The ˆXL operator thus manipulates one of the two\ndegrees of freedom of the array in Fig."
