    "full": "of stabilizers, |ψX ⟩ is not trivially related\nto |ψ⟩ (unless |ψ⟩ is an eigenstate of XL, namely |+L⟩ or\n|−L⟩). The ˆXL operator thus manipulates one of the two\ndegrees of freedom of the array in Fig. 3.\nThe other degree of freedom in the array can be manipulated by constructing a ˆZL operator using a completely\nanalogous product of ˆZ data qubit operations: We again\nbuild a chain of operations using ˆZ operators that are\npaired across each measure-X qubit that would otherwise\nreport a measurement change. The chain of paired ˆZ operators must cross the entire array, and further must start\nand end on a Z-boundary (rather than the X-boundaries\non either end of the ˆXL chain). An example of such a\nchain is ˆZL = ˆZ6 ˆZ7 ˆZ3 ˆZ8 ˆZ9 in Fig. 3. This ˆZL operator chain commutes with all the stabilizers in the array,\nand thus when operating on a quiescent state, generates\na new quiescent state |ψZ⟩ = ˆZL|ψ⟩ with the same measurement outcomes as |ψ⟩. The state |ψZ⟩ clearly diﬀers\nfrom |ψ⟩, as we have phase-ﬂipped ﬁve data qubits, and\nas ˆZL cannot be written as a product of stabilizers, |ψZ⟩\nis not trivially related to |ψ⟩; ˆZL thus manipulates a second degree of freedom in the array. We show below that\nˆXL and ˆZL do not commute, so they manipulate two independent degrees of freedom just as for a physical qubit.\nIt may seem that we could choose other chains of single qubit operator products to deﬁne diﬀerent ˆXL or\nˆZL operators."
