    "full": "includes superposition states of the un-bit-ﬂipped and\nthe bit-ﬂipped states of the data qubits involved in ˆXloop;\nthis holds for any operator that is equal to the product\nof one or more stabilizers. 11\nThe same argument applies to alternative ˆZ ′\nL operators: Any such operator is equal to the original ˆZL multiplied by a product of ˆZ stabilizers, and thus any ˆZ ′\nL|ψ⟩\ndiﬀers from ˆZL|ψ⟩ by ±1. Hence there is also only one\nlinearly independent ˆZL operator for this array.\nWe now show that the two operators ˆXL and ˆZL satisfy the critical anti-commutation relation in Eq. (1). We\nhave\nˆXL ˆZL =\n(\nˆX1 ˆX2 ˆX3 ˆX4 ˆX5\n) (\nˆZ9 ˆZ10 ˆZ3 ˆZ11 ˆZ12\n)\n= ˆX3 ˆZ3\n(\nˆX1 ˆX2 ˆX4 ˆX5\n) (\nˆZ9 ˆZ10 ˆZ11 ˆZ12\n)\n= − ˆZ3 ˆX3\n(\nˆZ9 ˆZ10 ˆZ11 ˆZ12\n) (\nˆX1 ˆX2 ˆX4 ˆX5\n)\n= − ˆZL ˆXL,\n(9)\nwhere all the single qubit operations are on diﬀerent\nqubits, and thus commute, except for ˆX3 and ˆZ3, which\nanti-commute, ˆX3 ˆZ3 = − ˆZ3 ˆX3. This makes ˆXL and ˆZL\nanti-commute. The logical operators ˆXL and ˆZL therefore have exactly the same commutation relation as the\nphysical qubit operators ˆX and ˆZ. You can show that\nˆX 2\nL = ˆZ 2\nL = ˆI. We can also construct the logical operator ˆYL = ˆZL ˆXL, comprising two chains of ˆZ and ˆX\n11 In other words, if |ψ ⟩ includes qubits 2, 10, 11 and 12 in the state\n|... +−++... ⟩, then |ψ ⟩ must also include the state where these\nqubits are in |... − + − −... ⟩, i.e. |ψ ⟩ = ±|... + − + +... ⟩ ±\n|... − + − −... ⟩ +... ."
