    "full": "ger performs the surface code cycle of CNOTs\nfollowed by measurement).\nIn Fig. 9 we show how this can be done, by turning\noﬀ a single measure-Z qubit and creating a hole, which\nwe call a “Z-cut hole”. Turning oﬀ the measure-Z qubit\nmeans that we no longer measure its stabilizer, which\ncreates two additional degrees of freedom in the surface\ncode array. We can manipulate these degrees of freedom\nin a similar fashion to the way we manipulate the array\nqubit, by deﬁning logical operators ˆXL and ˆZL that anticommute. We will call this logical qubit a “single Z-cut\nqubit”.\nWe have positioned the Z-cut hole near an X boundary of the array. Turning oﬀ the ˆZ stabilizer leaves a\nsmall hole surrounded by measure-X qubits, i.e. this\ncreates an internal X boundary. We deﬁne the operator ˆXL = ˆX1 ˆX2 ˆX3, which connects the array’s outer X\nboundary with the internal X boundary of the hole. As\ncan be seen, this logical operator comprises a chain of\noperators that are paired across each ˆZ stabilizer, so it\ncommutes with all the ˆZ stabilizers in the array, and trivially commutes with the ˆX stabilizers as well. We also\ndeﬁne ˆZL = ˆZ3 ˆZ4 ˆZ5 ˆZ6, a set of data qubit ˆZ operators\nthat form a loop around the Z-cut hole; the ˆZ operators\nare paired across each ˆX stabilizer, commuting with each\nXXXX\nX X\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nZ6\nZ5\nZ3\nZ4\nX1\nX3\nX2 XL\nZL\nFIG. 9."
