    "full": "commuting with each\nXXXX\nX X\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nZ\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nZ6\nZ5\nZ3\nZ4\nX1\nX3\nX2 XL\nZL\nFIG. 9. (Color online) A single Z-cut qubit. The array boundary is indicated by a solid black line (top), while the other\nthree sides of the array continue outwards indeﬁnitely. Two\nlogical operators, ˆXL = ˆX1 ˆX2 ˆX3 from the array’s outer X\nboundary to the internal X boundary of the Z-cut hole, and\nˆZL = ˆZ3 ˆZ4 ˆZ5 ˆZ6 surrounding the Z-cut hole, are shown. Note\nthat the operator chains for ˆXL and ˆZL have one physical data\nqubit in common (data qubit 3).\nof these, and trivially with all the ˆZ stabilizers as well.\nThis operator loop is not constrained by a ˆZ stabilizer as\nit would be without the Z-cut hole, so it can change the\nquiescent state |ψ⟩ in a non-trivial way.\nThe ˆXL chain and the ˆZL loop share one data qubit,\nnumber 3 in Fig. 9. These two operators therefore anticommute:\nˆXL ˆZL = ( ˆX1 ˆX2 ˆX3)( ˆZ3 ˆZ4 ˆZ5 ˆZ6)\n= ˆZ4 ˆZ5 ˆZ6( ˆX3 ˆZ3) ˆX1 ˆX2\n= − ˆZ4 ˆZ5 ˆZ6( ˆZ3 ˆX3) ˆX1 ˆX2\n= − ˆZL ˆXL.\n(14)\nYou can show that ˆX 2\nL = ˆZ 2\nL = ˆI, and we can deﬁne\nˆYL = ˆZL ˆXL. Just as with the 2D array, we have created\na set of anti-commuting operators associated with the\nZ-cut hole that satisfy all the requirements for a logical\nqubit, so the Z-cut hole is indeed a logical qubit."
