    "full": "ay. We can manipulate each\nZ-cut hole separately by deﬁning ˆXL1 and ˆZL1 for the\nupper Z-cut hole, and ˆXL2 and ˆZL2 for the lower Z-cut\nhole, in a way completely analogous to the single Z-cut\nqubit. These four linearly independent operators manipulate all four degrees of freedom, with each logical operator pair ( ˆXLj, ˆZLj) commuting with the other pair but\nanti-commuting with each other.\nWe can manipulate the two qubit holes in a correlated\nfashion by replacing ˆXL1 by the product ˆXL1 ˆXL2; this\noperator product anti-commutes with both ˆZL1 and ˆZL2,\nand together with ˆXL2 provides the same functionality\nas ˆXL1 and ˆXL2 (note that we can recover ˆXL1 by multiplying the operator product by ˆXL2, using ˆX 2\nL2 = ˆIL).\nNow, we can deﬁne a new ˆXL operator linking the two\nqubit holes, shown in Fig. 10; this operator is the product\nof three data qubit ˆX operators, and links the internal ˆX\nboundary of the upper qubit to the internal ˆX boundary\nof the lower qubit. If you examine the ﬁgure, you will see\nthat the product ˆXL ˆXL1 ˆXL2 is equal to the product of all\nthe stabilizers enclosed by these operators (these stabilizers have black outlines in Fig. 10)."
