    "full": "can\nreplace ˆXL1 (dashed blue line) by the product ˆXL1 ˆXL2, which\nperforms an ˆXL bit ﬂip of both qubit holes, and along with\nˆXL2 provides equivalent functionality to the two separate ˆX\noperators. However, the product ˆXL1 ˆXL2 can be multiplied\nby all the outlined ˆX stabilizers, which at most give a sign\nchange to the operator, to yield the equivalent operator ˆXL\n(solid blue line) that links the two qubit holes.\ntors must be local, to be able to perform logical gates in\nparallel. Consequently, we choose to use ˆXL along with\neither ˆZL1 or ˆZL2. If we choose to use ˆZL2 ≡ ˆZL, and use\nthis pair to deﬁne ˆYL = ˆZL ˆXL, we have a complete set\n{ ˆXL, ˆZL, ˆYL} of logical qubit operators for this double\nZ-cut qubit. By restricting ourselves to this set of logical\noperators, we are changing the two qubit holes in a correlated fashion, so that eﬀectively we are manipulating\na single two-level logical qubit; we will write the state\nof this composite logical qubit in the usual way, as e.g.\nα|gL⟩ +β|eL⟩. Note that in more advanced work [46],\nmore general defect and logical operator conﬁgurations\nare permitted.\nWe can create a double X-cut qubit in a similar way\nto the double Z-cut qubit, by turning oﬀ two measure-X\nqubits. Both the double Z-cut and double X-cut qubit\ntypes are shown in Fig. 11."
