    "full": "is example uses a number\nof results that we have not introduced; the reader may\nwant to return to this example after reading the discussion of the Heisenberg representation of the CNOT in\nSect. XIV C. We represent the two-qubit CNOT operation with the two-qubit operator ˆCL, which transforms\ntwo-qubit operators such as ˆIL⊗ ˆZL according to the rules\ndescribed there (so e.g. ˆCL( ˆIL ⊗ ˆZL) = ( ˆZL ⊗ ˆZL) ˆCL).\nConsider the circuit fragment shown in Fig. 12a, in\nwhich a ˆZL operator on logical qubit 1 and the identity\nˆIL operator on qubit 2 is followed by a logical Hadamard\non qubit 1, a logical CNOT in which qubit 1 is the control\nand qubit 2 the target, and a second logical Hadamard on\nqubit 1, with the circuit terminated by ˆXL measurements\nof both logical qubits.\nRather than actually applying the ˆZL operator to qubit\n1, the ˆZL operator is instead commuted through the\nHadamard, which transforms ˆZL to ˆXL (using ˆHL ˆZL =\nˆXL ˆHL; see e.g. [2]). This is shown in Fig. 12b. The ˆXL\non qubit 1 and ˆIL on qubit 2 are then commuted through\nthe CNOT, using ˆCL( ˆXL ⊗ ˆIL) = ( ˆXL ⊗ ˆXL) ˆCL, leaving\nˆXL on qubit 1 and ˆXL on qubit 2 after the CNOT, as\nshown in Fig. 12c. Commuting the ﬁrst qubit ˆXL through\nthe second Hadamard, this becomes ˆZL on qubit 1 with\nˆXL on qubit 2 (Fig. 12d). We have now reached the terminal measurements at the end of the circuit. At this\n--- [Page 18] ---\n18\n〉\n〉\n(a)\n〉\n〉\n(b)\nIL\nIL\nXL\n〉\n〉\n(c) XL\nXL\n〉\n〉\n(d) ZL\nXL\n〉\n〉\n(e) -\nFIG. 12."
