    "id": "fowler_2012_surface_codes.pdf_c82",
    "doc": "Fowler 2012 Surface Codes",
    "snippet": "ng the upper qubit hole, the initial state\nof the qubit corresponds to the measurement outcome of\nthe upper measure-X qubit just before it is turned oﬀ,\ni.e. |+L⟩ if Xabcd = +1 and |−L⟩ if Xabcd = −1. If\nthe initial state is not the desired one, we can apply a\nˆZL phase-ﬂip to that logical qubit; as discussed above,\nthis phase-ﬂip would be applied “in software”, meaning it\nwould not actually be applied, but would instead be commuted through the quantum circuit until it is canceled\nby a second op...",
    "full": "ng the upper qubit hole, the initial state\nof the qubit corresponds to the measurement outcome of\nthe upper measure-X qubit just before it is turned oﬀ,\ni.e. |+L⟩ if Xabcd = +1 and |−L⟩ if Xabcd = −1. If\nthe initial state is not the desired one, we can apply a\nˆZL phase-ﬂip to that logical qubit; as discussed above,\nthis phase-ﬂip would be applied “in software”, meaning it\nwould not actually be applied, but would instead be commuted through the quantum circuit until it is canceled\nby a second operator or the logical qubit is measured.\nThe alternative to initializing the X-cut logical qubit\nin the ˆXL basis is to perform a “diﬃcult” initialization\nin the ˆZL basis, i.e. in either |gL⟩ or |eL⟩. This can be\ndone using a logical Hadamard after initializing in the ˆXL\nbasis. Logical Hadamards are however somewhat complicated (see Section XV), so it is simpler to initialize in the\nˆZL basis directly. This is illustrated for an X-cut qubit\nin Fig. 14. Brieﬂy, starting with a fully stabilized array,\na strip of ˆX stabilizers is turned oﬀ, where each end of\n16 There is an important distinction between what are called “Clifford gates” and “non-Cliﬀord gates”; the set of logical gates we\nare developing here, ˆXL, ˆZL, ˆHL, ˆSL and ˆS†\nL, ˆTL and ˆT †\nL, and\nthe two-qubit logical CNOT, are all Cliﬀord gates except ˆTL\nand ˆT †\nL (see Ref. [47]). We discuss how to commute ˆXL and ˆZL\nthrough the ˆSL and ˆTL gates in Sect. XVI.\n--- [Page 19] ---\n19\n(a) (b)\nZL\nXL\nFIG. 13."
