    "full": "easure-Z qubits back\nto 4-terminal measurements, leaving us with a logical qubit\ninitialized in |gL⟩ due to step (c).\n“easy” measurement of ˆZL or a “diﬃcult” measurement\nof ˆXL for a Z-cut qubit.\nXI. ERRORS DURING ST ABILIZER\nMANIPULA TIONS\nOur initialization and measurement procedures, especially the “diﬃcult” ones, involve turning stabilizers\non and oﬀ, switching stabilizers from four- to threeterminal measurements and back, measuring individual\ndata qubits, and re-setting (re-initializing) data qubits\nto eigenstates of ˆX or ˆZ. These processes are not used\njust for initialization and measurement, but also form the\nheart of the topologically-implemented CNOT operation\nas well as the logical Hadamard. These manipulations\nwould seem to allow errors to occur in some undetectable\n(a) (b)\n(c) (d)\nMZ\nMZ\nMZ\ng\ng\ng\nXL\nZL\nFIG. 15. (Color online) Z-axis (“diﬃcult”) measurement process for an X-cut logical qubit. We start (a) with an X-cut\nqubit, for which we display the logical operators ˆXL and ˆZL,\nand begin the measurement (b) by turning oﬀ the measure-X\nqubits between the two qubit cuts while also switching the\nneighboring measure-Z qubits from four- to three-terminal\nmeasurements. We measure the un-stabilized data qubits\nalong ˆZ. The product of the measurement outcomes is the\nmeasurement of ˆZL. A pending ˆZL operator would have no\neﬀect, while a pending ˆXL would be used to reverse the sign\nof this measurement outcome."
