    "full": "the former corresponding\nto a Z-cut qubit and the latter an X-cut).\nThe process to make a larger Z-cut qubit is outlined in\nFig. 16; a completely analogous process is used to make\nlarger X-cut qubits. Details are given in the ﬁgure caption and in Appendix E.\nThe process shown in Fig. 16 initializes the qubit in an\neigenstate of ˆZL, with an eigenvalue equal to the product of the stable measurement outcomes of the four ˆZ\nstabilizers in the cut. Initializing the qubit instead in an\nˆXL eigenstate can be done using a procedure analogous\nto that described in Section X.\nMeasuring a larger qubit is also similar to the procedure for measuring small qubits. If we want to measure\nthe logical qubit in Fig. 16 along ˆZL, an “easy” measurement, we turn on the four ˆZ and the one ˆX stabilizer in\nthe hole; the ˆZL eigenvalue is equal to the stable product\nof the four ˆZ stabilizers,Zs1Zs2Zs3Zs4 = ±1 (where e.g.\nZs1 = Z1,abcd is the measurement outcome of the stabilizer ˆZs1). If we want instead to measure the logical qubit\nalong ˆXL, a “diﬃcult” measurement, we follow a procedure analogous to that for the diﬃcult measurement of\na small qubit, shown in Fig. 15: We open the strip of ˆZ\nstabilizers through which the ˆXL chain passes, turn the\n--- [Page 22] ---\n22\nXL\nZL\nMX\nMXZs1\nZs2 Zs3\nZs4\nXs1\n1\n2\n3\n4 5\n6\n7\n8\nMXMX\nFIG. 16. (Color online) Z-cut qubit with a ﬁve-stabilizer hole,\nshowing only the upper qubit multi-cell hole."
