    "doc": "Fowler 2012 Surface Codes",
    "snippet": "in the surface code array. We will discuss later how\nthe single logical qubit gates ˆSL, ˆTL and the Hadamard\nˆHL, all required to perform quantum algorithms, can be\nperformed on these logical qubits. We turn now to the\nvery interesting method used to entangle logical qubits.\nThis can be done in a very elegant way by moving the\nlogical qubit holes around on the 2D array, providing a\ncentral functionality of the surface code. By moving one\nlogical qubit hole between the two holes of a second logi...",
    "full": "in the surface code array. We will discuss later how\nthe single logical qubit gates ˆSL, ˆTL and the Hadamard\nˆHL, all required to perform quantum algorithms, can be\nperformed on these logical qubits. We turn now to the\nvery interesting method used to entangle logical qubits.\nThis can be done in a very elegant way by moving the\nlogical qubit holes around on the 2D array, providing a\ncentral functionality of the surface code. By moving one\nlogical qubit hole between the two holes of a second logical qubit, thus “braiding” the logical qubits together, we\ncan perform a logical CNOT. We ﬁrst however need to\nlearn how to move a logical qubit hole.\nMoving a logical qubit is best described in two stages,\nﬁrst outlining what we physically do to the surface code\n20 In our initial description, we only required the ability to measure\nqubits along ˆZ; an ˆX measurement in addition would be useful\nhere, but the equivalent can be performed by ﬁrst performing a\nHadamard operation on the qubit, followed by a ˆZ measurement.\narray, and second describing how we transform the logical\noperators ˆXL and ˆZL, in a way that preserves their identity while matching what is happening to the data qubits\nin the array. The transformations of the logical operators\nare described in the context of the Heisenberg representation, so we start by brieﬂy reviewing the Heisenberg\nrepresentation of quantum mechanics (see e.g."
