    "full": "qubit 6 (using either an idle measure-Z qubit, or directly measuring the data qubit, as shown). (c) We deﬁne the extended operator ˆX ′\nL by multiplying ˆXL by ˆX6:\nˆX ′\nL = ˆX6 ˆXL = ˆX1 ˆX2 ˆX3 ˆX6. We now turn on the ˆZ stabilizer\njust above qubit 6; we wait d surface code cycles in order to\nproperly establish the value of this stabilizer. We deﬁne ˆZ ′\nL\nas the product of this ˆZ stabilizer and ˆZ e\nL: ˆZ ′\nL = ˆZ6 ˆZ7 ˆZ8 ˆZ9.\nsee Fig. 17b.\nWe next complete the second surface code cycle of the\nmove, turning the original qubit hole stabilizer back on.\nWe redeﬁne ˆZ e\nL by multiplying it by the four ˆZ operators that make up the stabilizer that was just turned on,\nˆZ3 ˆZ4 ˆZ5 ˆZ6, giving\nˆZ ′\nL = ( ˆZ3 ˆZ4 ˆZ5 ˆZ6) × ˆZ e\nL\n= ( ˆZ3 ˆZ4 ˆZ5 ˆZ6) × ( ˆZ3 ˆZ4 ˆZ5 ˆZ7 ˆZ8 ˆZ9)\n= ˆZ6 ˆZ7 ˆZ8 ˆZ9.\n(19)\nThe two new logical operators ˆZ ′\nL and ˆX ′\nL are now as\ndrawn in Fig. 17c. Note the new logical operators still\nshare a single data qubit and commute with all the stabilizers, so these continue to deﬁne a logical qubit. The\nprocess of moving the qubit hole involves operations similar to those used for the “easy” and “diﬃcult” initializations, so error processes are as discussed in Sect. XI.\n--- [Page 24] ---\n24\nB."
