    "snippet": "ivalent to Eq. (20)\nas\n|qL⟩ → ˆX ′pZ\nL ˆZ ′pX\nL |q′\nL⟩, (21)\nwhere |q′\nL⟩ is the desired logical state.\nC. Multi-cell logical qubit move\nWe can easily generalize the one-cell move to a multicell move. A multi-cell move can be done in the same\nnumber of surface code clock cycles as a one-cell move,\nand we can translate the logical qubit hole over an unlimited number of cells. Multi-cell moves are performed\n(a) \nLXˆ\nLZˆ\niZ s2\niZ s3\ni\ns,nZ …\nLXˆ\ne\nLZˆ\neX 1\neX 2\ne\nn-1X …\nfZ s2\nf\ns, n-1Z\nfZs1…\nLXˆ\nLZ...",
    "full": "ivalent to Eq. (20)\nas\n|qL⟩ → ˆX ′pZ\nL ˆZ ′pX\nL |q′\nL⟩, (21)\nwhere |q′\nL⟩ is the desired logical state.\nC. Multi-cell logical qubit move\nWe can easily generalize the one-cell move to a multicell move. A multi-cell move can be done in the same\nnumber of surface code clock cycles as a one-cell move,\nand we can translate the logical qubit hole over an unlimited number of cells. Multi-cell moves are performed\n(a) \nLXˆ\nLZˆ\niZ s2\niZ s3\ni\ns,nZ …\nLXˆ\ne\nLZˆ\neX 1\neX 2\ne\nn-1X …\nfZ s2\nf\ns, n-1Z\nfZs1…\nLXˆ\nLZˆ\n’\n’\n(b) (c) \nFIG. 18. (Color online) Process for moving a Z-cut logical\nqubit hole through multiple cells, using a simpliﬁed representation where the ˆXL chain is a blue (light) line linking\nthe logical qubit holes and passing through the data qubits\n(open circles), and the ˆZL loop is in red (gray). The upper (gray) and lower (white) Z-cut holes enclose idle ˆZ stabilizers; the outlines of these holes have measure-X qubits on\nthe vertices with data qubits on the edges. (a) Initial state,\nshowing the logical operators ˆXL and ˆZL, with the initial\n(pre-move) software-corrected ˆZ stabilizer measurement outcomesZ i\ns2, Z i\ns3...Z i\ns,n. The ˆZ stabilizers involved in the next\nstep are shown with thin black outlines; the jth stabilizer ˆZs,j\nis the stabilizer cell marked by that stabilizer’s initial measurement outcome Z i\ns,j."
