    "full": "s occurring when an X-cut logical\nqubit is initialized in a ˆZL eigenstate. A very similar discussion applies to errors that occur during the\nmove transformations, with some diﬀerences in the details. The data qubits that are left isolated during the\nmove can suﬀer from both ˆZ and ˆX errors; ˆX errors have\nno impact, as they are erased by the single data qubit ˆX\nmeasurements during the move. ˆZ errors will be detected\nby computing the product of each data qubit ˆX measurement with its corresponding three-terminal ˆX stabilizer\nmeasurement, and comparing this product with that stabilizer’s prior four-terminal ˆX measurement (this is how\nˆX errors were handled in Sect. XI). ˆZ errors on the data\nqubits bordering the cut are detected and localized by the\ntwo ˆX stabilizers that monitor each of these data qubits.\nAn ˆX error on one of these data qubits changes the sign\nof the solitary ˆZ stabilizer that monitors that data qubit,\nbut this error will persist in time, and can thus be distinguished from a stabilizer measurement error by waiting\nd surface code cycles.\nXIV. THE BRAIDING TRANSFORMA TION\nAND THE LOGICAL CNOT\nA braiding transformation is a particularly important\ntype of move. A braid comprises a pair of multi-cell\nmoves involving one of the two holes in a double-cut logical qubit."
