    "full": "aid transformation involving just one\nlogical qubit are given in the captions for Figs. 19 and 20,\nwith a discussion of the sign changes and byproduct operators given in Appendix H. Note that in Figs. 19 and 20,\nwhile we separate the actions that aﬀect the ˆXL operator\nfrom those that aﬀect ˆZL, all ˆX measurements of the isolated data qubits and measurements of the ˆZ stabilizers\nare performed as part of the braid, independent of what\nlogical operators are involved in the transformation, just\nas they are for the one-cell and multi-cell moves.\nB. Braiding two qubits\nWe now turn to the less trivial situation where the\nbraid path encloses another logical qubit hole. The easiest way to understand this is to examine how operators\non the two logical qubits are transformed by the braid.\nThe two-qubit operators will be outer products of the\nsingle-qubit operators ˆXL, ˆZL and ˆIL. The ˆYL operator\nis the product of ˆZL and ˆXL, and its transformation can\nbe understood in terms of the ˆXL and ˆZL transformations.\nWe quickly review two-qubit operators, using as an\nexample ˆXL ⊗ ˆZL. We write the two-qubit logical state as\n|aLbL⟩, where |aL⟩ represents the state of the ﬁrst logical\nqubit and |bL⟩ that of the second qubit. The notation\nˆXL ⊗ ˆZL means that ˆXL acts on the ﬁrst qubit state\n|aL⟩, and ˆZL acts on the second qubit state |bL⟩."
