    "full": "action of the CNOT. This would\nseem to involve showing that the CNOT performs the\ncorrect transformation for all sixteen outer products of\npairs of the four single qubit operators, ˆI, ˆX, ˆY and\nˆZ. It turns out you only need to show that the CNOT\ntransforms four of these outer products correctly:\nˆC †\n(\nˆI ⊗ ˆX\n)\nˆC = ˆI ⊗ ˆX, (31)\nˆC †\n(\nˆX ⊗ ˆI\n)\nˆC = ˆX ⊗ ˆX, (32)\nˆC †\n(\nˆI ⊗ ˆZ\n)\nˆC = ˆZ ⊗ ˆZ, and (33)\nˆC †\n(\nˆZ ⊗ ˆI\n)\nˆC = ˆZ ⊗ ˆI. (34)\nThe other twelve relations are either trivial ( ˆI ⊗ ˆI transforms to itself), or can be written in terms of these four\ntransformations. Hence ˆC †( ˆX ⊗ ˆX) ˆC = ˆX ⊗ ˆI is the same\nas Eq. (32), as can be seen by multiplying both sides of\nEq. (32) by ˆC † on the left and by ˆC on the right, and\nusing the fact that ˆC is unitary. Combinations such as\nˆX ⊗ ˆZ transform according to\nˆC †\n(\nˆX ⊗ ˆZ\n)\nˆC = ˆC †\n(\nˆX ⊗ ˆI\n) (\nˆI ⊗ ˆZ\n)\nˆC\n= ˆC †\n(\nˆX ⊗ ˆI\n)\nˆC ˆC †\n(\nˆI ⊗ ˆZ\n)\nˆC\n=\n(\nˆX ⊗ ˆX\n) (\nˆZ ⊗ ˆZ\n)\n= ˆX ˆZ ⊗ ˆX ˆZ\n= ˆY ⊗ ˆY.\n(35)\nOther combinations involving ˆY can be worked out using\nthe identity ˆY = ˆZ ˆX; hence\nˆC †\n(\nˆY ⊗ ˆI\n)\nˆC = ˆC †\n(\nˆZ ˆX ⊗ ˆI\n)\nˆC\n= ˆC †\n(\nˆZ ⊗ ˆI\n) (\nˆX ⊗ ˆI\n)\nˆC\n= ˆC †\n(\nˆZ ⊗ ˆI\n)\nˆC ˆC †\n(\nˆX ⊗ ˆI\n)\nˆC\n=\n(\nˆZ ⊗ ˆI\n) (\nˆX ⊗ ˆX\n)\n= ˆZ ˆX ⊗ ˆI ˆX\n= ˆY ⊗ ˆX.\n(36)\nWe can therefore validate a CNOT implementation\nby verifying that it satisﬁes the four relations given by\n--- [Page 30] ---\n30\nEqs. (31-34)."
