    "full": "ementations of the ˆSL and ˆTL operators and\ntheir adjoints. These operators are represented in the\nstandard |gL⟩, |eL⟩ basis by the 2 × 2 matrices\nˆSL =\n(\n1 0\n0 i\n)\n, (39)\nand\nˆTL =\n( 1 0\n0 eiπ/4\n)\n. (40)\nThe ˆSL gate is also sometimes called the P or phase gate,\nand ˆTL is also called the π/8 gate. 23\nWe note the important identities\nˆTL ˆTL = ˆSL (41)\nand\nˆSL ˆSL = ˆZL. (42)\nWe also have the identities\nˆTL = ˆSL ˆT †\nL, (43)\nand correspondingly\nˆT †\nL = ˆS†\nL ˆTL = ˆZL ˆSL ˆTL. (44)\nAs we will see, the circuit used to implement ˆTL is probabilistic, and half the time generates ˆT †\nL. From Eq. (43),\nwhen this occurs ˆT †\nL can be converted to ˆTL by application of an ˆSL gate. ˆTL can be similarly converted to ˆT †\nL\n23 The π/ 8 name originates from deﬁning ˆTL by the matrix in Eq.\n(40) multiplied by an overall (and unimportant) phase factor of\ne−iπ/8.\n--- [Page 33] ---\n33\nXL\nZL\n(a) (b)\n(c)\n(e) (f)\n(d)\nMX\nMX\nXL\nZL\nXL\nZL\nXL\nZL\nXL\nZL\nXL\nZL\nFIG. 27. (Color online) (a) In the ﬁrst surface code cycle for the Hadamard, a ring o f ˆX stabilizers surrounding the target\nlogical qubit is turned oﬀ, and the four-terminal ˆZ stabilizers on the ring’s borders are reduced to three- and two-terminal\nmeasurements. The data qubits isolated within the ring itself are measured in the ˆZ basis to maintain error tracking with\nthe reduced-size ˆZ stabilizers. This stabilizer measurement pattern is applied three times (three is speciﬁc to the distance- 7\nqubits used here; see Ref. [49])."
