    "full": "ˆZL ˆS†\nL|ψ L⟩ where ˆZL is a byproduct operator.\ntions. An input state |ψL⟩ in one logical qubit is deterministically transformed into ˆSL|ψL⟩ by interacting\nwith the ancilla qubit in the |YL⟩ state. This can be\nmost easily seen by testing the circuit with the input\nstate |ψL⟩ = α|g⟩ + β|e⟩, where we drop the L subscript for simplicity. We consider the states |g⟩ and\n|e⟩ of |ψL⟩ separately. For |g⟩, the CNOT does nothing, so the |YL⟩ ancilla state transforms according to\nˆH ˆH|YL⟩ = |YL⟩. For |e⟩, the CNOT performs an ˆXL\non the ancilla state, which then transforms according to\nˆH ˆX ˆH ˆX|YL⟩ = ˆZ ˆX|YL⟩ = −|e⟩ +i|g⟩, where we have\ndropped normalization. Writing the circuit state as |ab⟩\nwhere a represents the input state and b the ancilla, the\ncircuit produces the output\nα|g⟩(|g⟩ +i|e⟩) +β|e⟩(−|e⟩ +i|g⟩) (47)\n=α|gg⟩ +iα|ge⟩ +iβ|eg⟩ −β|ee⟩ (48)\n= (α|g⟩ +iβ|e⟩)(|g⟩ +i|e⟩) (49)\n= ( ˆSL|ψL⟩)|YL⟩, (50)\nas desired.\nTo instead apply ˆS†\nL, we use the identity ˆS†\nL = ˆZL ˆSL,\nwhich means we use the circuit shown in Fig. 29 and have\na byproduct ˆZL appear on the output, in other words\n|ψL⟩ transforms to ˆZL( ˆS†\nL|ψL⟩).\nThe ˆTL gate is implemented with the non-deterministic\ncircuit shown in Fig. 30. Given the input ancilla state\n|θL⟩ = |gL⟩ +eiθ|eL⟩, the output |φL⟩ of this circuit is\n|φL⟩ = ˆX pZ\nL ˆRZ ((−1)pZθ) |ψL⟩. (51)\nThe ﬁrst operator is a byproduct operator, whose power\npZ is equal to 0(1) if the ˆZL measurement MZ of the\nlogical qubit state is +1( −1)."
