    "full": "cuit for the ˆTL gate. The circuit uses the\nancilla state |AL⟩ = ( |gL⟩ +eiπ/4|eL⟩)/\n√\n2, which is used to\ncontrol a CNOT on the target state |ψ L⟩, transforming the\nancilla to the output state |φ L⟩. The CNOT target state is\nmeasured along ˆZL, resulting in a projective, probabilistic\noutcome. Following this, the CNOT control qubit is processed by a conditional ˆSL gate: If the ˆZL measurementMZ\nyields a +1 outcome, the output state is the desired one,\n|φ L⟩ = ˆTL|ψ L⟩, and the ˆSL gate is not applied. If however the measurement yields a −1 outcome, the output is\n|φ L⟩ = ˆXL ˆT †\nL and the ˆSL gate is applied, resulting in the output ˆXL ˆZL ˆTL|ψ L⟩. Note the double lines represent the classical measurement data with a probabilistic outcome, with\nthese data controlling the ˆSL gate. The bracketed operators\nin the output correspond to MZ = +1 ( ˆIL) and MZ = −1\n( ˆXL ˆZL).\nthe circuit output ˆTL|ψL⟩ by applying ˆSL, as ˆSL ˆTL =\nˆZL ˆT †\nL; this is done using the ˆSL circuit, with the output\nincluding the byproduct operator ˆZL that is handled by\nthe control software.\nA. Commuting ˆXL and ˆZL through ˆSL and ˆTL\nWe turn brieﬂy to the issue of commuting the ˆXL and\nˆZL operators through the ˆSL and ˆTL gates, which is necessary if the classical control hardware is to handle all\nˆXL and ˆZL operations, as discussed in Sect. IX. The\ncommutation relations are most easily worked out using\nthe circuits that implement ˆSL and ˆTL, Figs."
