    "full": "erator, we have ˆXL ⊗ ˆXL →\nˆXL ⊗ ˆZL. The second CNOT performs the transformation ˆXL ⊗ ˆZL → ˆYL ⊗ ˆYL, and the second Hadamard does\nnot aﬀect the second ˆYL (other than an unimportant sign\nchange). Hence ˆXL ⊗ ˆIL is transformed to ˆYL ⊗ ˆYL, and\nthe control software must note the byproduct operator\ntransformation ˆXL → ˆYL = ˆZL ˆXL on the output state,\nwith the two byproduct operators handled in software.\nAn additional ˆYL also appears on the ancilla |YL⟩, but as\nˆYL|YL⟩ = i|YL⟩, this does nothing other than the unimportant phase factor i, which can be ignored.\nIn summary then, the ˆSL gate commutes with ˆZL and\nˆXL as follows:\n{ ˆSL ˆZL → ˆZL ˆSL,\nˆSL ˆXL → ˆZL ˆXL ˆSL.\n(58)\nTo show that ˆZL and ˆTL commute, we use similar reasoning using the circuit in Fig. 30. As ˆTL also represents\na form of rotation about ˆZ, this makes sense. There is\na slight wrinkle when we get the measurement outcome\nMZ = −1, which means that a ˆSL gate will be applied,\nin this case to the state |φL⟩ = ˆXL ˆT †\nL( ˆZL|ψL⟩). Then we\nhave\nˆSL|φL⟩ = ˆSL ˆXL ˆT †\nL( ˆZL|ψL⟩)\n= ˆXL ˆS†\nL ˆZL ˆT †\nL|ψL⟩\n= ˆXL ˆZL ˆS†\nL ˆT †\nL|ψL⟩\n= ˆXL ˆZL ˆZL ˆSL ˆT †\nL|ψL⟩\n= ˆXL ˆTL|ψL⟩,\n(59)\nwhich is the desired result, with a byproduct operator\nˆXL that is handled by the control software.\nThe commutation of ˆXL through the ˆTL gate is similar. We take the input state ˆXL|ψL⟩, and commute\nˆXL through the CNOT with an implicit ˆIL operating on\nthe CNOT control qubit. The CNOT takes ˆIL ⊗ ˆXL →\nˆIL ⊗ ˆXL."
